From 72d18a9a5b5216c2e7971f7de952c5e4fcb818ff Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 02:51:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 6/8] ci: audit Python and Go dependencies for known
 vulnerabilities

Nothing checked the dependencies against published advisories. A new
dependency-audit job runs two pinned scanners:

- make audit runs pip-audit 2.10.1 from its own virtualenv against
  the .venv created by make install, i.e. the third-party versions
  that the dev environment and CI actually install. The editable
  forgemesh-* packages are not on PyPI and are skipped
  (--skip-editable); --strict cannot be combined with that, since it
  treats every skipped distribution as fatal.
- make -C gateway vulncheck runs govulncheck v1.9.0 on the gateway
  with the Go version from GO_VERSION. It reports only
  vulnerabilities in code the gateway calls, standard library
  included.

Both pass today. Python advisories without a fix, or assessed as not
affecting ForgeMesh, are listed explicitly in PIP_AUDIT_IGNORE with
the package, the reason and the removal condition (none so far).
govulncheck has no suppression mechanism, so that step stays red
until Go ships a fix or the call is avoided. SECURITY.md 3.1 now
documents this policy and drops the claim that dependency versions
are pinned in CI, which was never true.
